<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> a strata winter plan in Vancouver needs five things written down before the first flake: a priority map of what gets cleared first, salting triggers tied to forecast freezes and not just snowfall, response terms for multi-day events, the municipal deadline your contractor must meet, and a time-stamped record of every visit. Vancouver averages under 40 centimetres of snow a year, so the risk is rarely the snow. It is the ice that follows.</p>

<h2>Why Vancouver winters catch strata councils out</h2>
<p>Most years the city sees a handful of snow days, often weeks apart, with rain and mild temperatures in between. That pattern produces the most dangerous surface there is: wet snow that half-melts, then refreezes overnight into a sheet across an entrance or a set of stairs. Councils that budget for "a couple of plow visits" find the real work is salting on a Tuesday night when it did not snow at all.</p>
<p>The strata corporation is responsible for common property under the Strata Property Act, and the City of Vancouver's Street and Traffic By-law requires the owner of a property to clear snow and ice from bordering sidewalks by 10 a.m. the morning after a snowfall. Burnaby and Richmond have comparable requirements. A slip on an uncleared walkway is a claim against the strata, and the question that follows is always the same: what did you do, and when?</p>

<h2>1. The priority map</h2>
<p>Walk the property with your contractor before winter and agree, in writing, the order of work:</p>
<ul>
<li>Building entrances, exterior stairs and the routes from them to the street and to parking.</li>
<li>Bordering public sidewalks, to meet the bylaw deadline.</li>
<li>Mailboxes, garbage and recycling enclosures, and accessible parking stalls.</li>
<li>Drive aisles and visitor parking last, once people can move safely on foot.</li>
<li>Where snow will be piled: never on drains, fire hydrants, or the accessible route.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. Salting triggers, not just snow triggers</h2>
<p>The plan should say when de-icer goes down, and the answer should include nights with a forecast freeze after rain or thaw, even with no snow in the forecast. Pre-treating before a freeze uses less product and prevents the ice bonding to the surface; treating after means chipping. Ask what product is used near planted beds, because heavy rock salt runoff damages lawns and shrubs by spring.</p>

<h2>3. Response terms for real events</h2>
<p>A single clearing pass at 6 a.m. is not a plan when snow falls all day. Specify that the crew returns during an ongoing event, that entrances are re-cleared at the end of the day, and how the contractor communicates when conditions are beyond the plan (an ice storm, or a fall heavier than the equipment on site can handle).</p>

<h2>4. Records that protect the council</h2>
<p>Every clearing and salting visit should produce a time-stamped log entry and photos of the areas treated, kept by the contractor and available to the council or property manager on request. If a claim arrives in March about an incident in January, that record is the strata's defence. If it does not exist, the strata's position is whatever the claimant says it is.</p>

<h2>5. Who does what, and what it costs</h2>
<ul>
<li>Seasonal retainer versus per-visit billing. A retainer buys guaranteed response; per-visit is cheaper in a mild winter and more expensive in a hard one.</li>
<li>Whether winter service sits inside the year-round grounds contract. One crew that already knows the property is faster in the dark at 5 a.m.</li>
<li>Resident responsibilities: patios, private steps and balconies are usually the owner's, and the plan should say so.</li>
<li>What happens to the landscape after: spring repair of salt damage and plow scrapes should be part of the same contract.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-list">
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">Does a strata legally have to clear snow?</h3><p class="faq-answer">The strata corporation maintains common property, and municipal bylaws put the duty to clear bordering sidewalks on the property owner. In Vancouver the deadline is 10 a.m. the morning after a snowfall. Get the exact wording for your municipality and write it into the contract.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">Is salting alone enough in Vancouver?</h3><p class="faq-answer">For many events, yes, because accumulations are small. But salt stops working well below about minus 10, and a heavy wet fall still needs shovels and a blower. The plan should cover both.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">When should we set up the winter plan?</h3><p class="faq-answer">October. The site walk, priority map and product decisions should be done before the first frost, and the contract signed before the first forecast of snow.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">What records should we ask for?</h3><p class="faq-answer">A time-stamped log and photos for every clearing and salting visit, available on request. Ask a prospective contractor to show you last winter's log for another property.</p></div>
